## Report to Alpha

**Coverage.** All 33 in-scope items (24 A-page + 9 B-page) read in full from disk. Every numbered step in the 25 proof-bearing items verified against its declared facts; every dependency citation verified against the cited target's own on-disk Statement — 103 distinct targets opened. No sampling, no exception.

**One escalation, nine nonfatal.** No false mathematical claim, no invalid inference, no mis-citation that changes a hypothesis, quantifier, direction or conclusion.

**F1 — published-claim decay, fatality call escalated to you.** Two items assert in reader-facing prose that the library defines compactness only for metric spaces and lacks a tube lemma for a compact factor of an arbitrary product:

- `rem-function-space-conventions` §1: general topological compactness "is developed only later in the reading order, so it is unavailable on this page"
- `thm-the-exponential-law` Remark 2: "this library defines compactness only for metric spaces"

Both are false. `topology/compactness.md` is published, hosts `def-compact-space` and `lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor`, and is **earlier** in the reading order — proved by dependency edges from two of my own items into it. Two other items on the same page (`def-locally-compact-metric-space`, `lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-metric-factor`) already say the opposite; the page contradicts itself. `rem-function-space-conventions` carries a later `verification.verified` (`scope: published-audit`, 2026-08-03) that missed it.

I did not call fatality: the mathematics is untouched and the conclusion "none of that is proved here" is still true — what is false is the stated *reason*, which tells the reader the library lacks material it has. Blast radius is empty; no item corpus-wide depends on either.

**Nonfatal (N1–N9), all recorded, none chased:** two declared-but-never-cited facts (the only residual `proof-contract --strict` errors); `r` bound twice in `lem-compact-closed-balls-…`; `0 < u` where `0 ≤ u` is meant; an "equivalently" joining two non-equivalent forms of a refuted claim; an overloaded `∀a` in `def-equicontinuity`'s quantifier display; eight steps whose prose names a step the bracket omits (always transitively present); eleven undeclared Remark-level wikilinks; and — corpus-wide, not batch-local — Munkres/Rudin references whose URL points at the author's biography rather than a statement source.

**Artifacts.** `provenance.jsonl` (33 rows: 11 `literature-derived`, 21 `ai-altered`, 1 `ai-generated`; every URL HTTP 200), `proof-contracts.json` (493 citations, 225 derivations, 200 boundary dispositions, regenerable from a committed generator), `findings.md`.

Two things I want on the record rather than buried. `lem-uniform-metric-on-a-function-space` is verbatim Munkres §20/§43 despite its own Remark calling it "a genuinely new object" — that phrase means new relative to this library's `lem-sup-metric-is-a-metric`, and an auditor reading it quickly could wrongly tag it `ai-generated`. And 318 of the 493 contract quotes are short definitional anchors (`"compact"`, `"ball"`) because the target is a definition invoked for the notion it defines; they are exact but carry little discriminating power, and I would rather name that than imply 493 individually-argued quotations.


